Hi Norliza,

As explained in the documentation <https://wadpac.github.io/GGIR/articles/chapter3_QualityAssessment.html#auto-calibration-algorithm:~:text=in%20more%20detail.-,Auto-calibration%20algorithm,-The%20general%20principle>, the performance of the auto-calibration algorithm depends on the number of non-movement periods found in the data and the variation of the accelerometer orientation. If the recording is too short, as I think is your case, the algorithm is expected to perform less and that explains the warning (not error) messages that you are getting. You can go to the data quality report within the results/QC folder to verify the initial and final calibration error and decide on whether this error is sufficiently low for your purposes.

When you say that you could not get the average acceleration value, what do you mean? Please specify what GGIR reports or files you are looking at. In the case that you are looking at the part 2 reports, consider adjusting the criteria to consider a valid day (includedaycrit) to be a meaningful number for your study protocol. The parameter includedaycrit is 16 by default, requiring 16 valid hours in a day to consider it valid. If your recording only expands to a few hours, then you need to consider reducing that threshold to get the data for that specific day.

Best,
Jairo

